    "available_at": "2026-10-20T21:21:33.233650+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\n1975 Hurst/Olds Is Dressed To Impress, Packs W30 Power\n14 photos\nPhoto: MGM CLASSIC CARS/YouTube\nIntroduced in 1968, the Hurst/Olds is arguably the most iconic muscle car produced by GM's mid-priced division. The original Hurst/Olds remained in production for only two years but returned between 1972 and 1975, with additional revivals in 1979 and 1983-1984.\nThe Hurst/Olds is a rare classic regardless of the year. Although production increased significantly in 1975, sales never exceeded 3,500 examples per year. Overall, Oldsmobile sold only 16,537 Hurst-branded vehicles over nine model years.\nThe first-year 1968 version is arguably the rarest, with 515 units made. The 1969 Hurst was built in just 906 examples, while 1972 saw only 633 cars roll off the showroom floor. These are also the most desirable iterations of the Hurst/Olds, mainly because they were true muscle cars in terms of oomph.\nThe Hurst you see here left the assembly line in 1975, at a time when muscle cars were a thing of the past. In fact, this is the first iteration of the series equipped with catalytic converters. It was also the first based on the formal roofed Cutlass Supreme coupe (instead of the fastback S model) and the first with a T-top roof.\nDrivetrain-wise, the 1975 Hurst/Olds had the same powerplants as its predecessor. The car came standard with the W25 unit, which was a 350-cubic-inch (5.7-liter) small-block V8 rated at 170 horsepower. Output dropped from 180 horsepower in 1974.\nThe already iconic W30, which displaced a massive 455 cubic inches (7.5 liters), was on the options list and delivered 190 horsepower. The mill was rated at 230 horsepower the previous year. The engines mated with Turbo Hydra-Matic gearboxes, with the W30 featuring a heavy-duty version.\nAlthough it may not be the most potent Hurst/Olds out there, this 1975 coupe is a noteworthy classic, mainly because it looks like it just left the assembly line. There's no info on whether it was restored, but it sure looks like it got a proper overhaul in recent years. It has shiny black paint, a pristine vinyl top, and squeaky-clean white upholstery. Needless to say, the W30 engine is also detailed to nearly as-new condition.\nThis Olds is also pretty rare. 1975 was the first year when production exceeded 2,000 units. Specifically, the company delivered 2,535 cars. Oldsmobile offered two colors, and records show this coupe is one of 1,242 examples painted Ebony Black (the other 1,293 were finished in Cameo White).\nAs for the engine, you're looking at one of 1,193 examples optioned up with the 455-cubic-inch W30 V8. There's no info on how many of these cars were black, but the statistic above indicates a near 50-50 split. All told, it might be one of fewer than 600 units with this drivetrain/color combo.\nIf you're looking for a restored Hurst/Olds that doesn't brake the bank, and you're fine with the low output, this coupe is currently waiting for a new owner in the MGM Classic Cars showroom . It's priced at $36,995.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nclassic car Oldsmobile hurst olds 1975 hurst olds Hurst Coupe\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Unrestored 1972 Cadillac Eldorado Rocks the Biggest V8 GM Ever Made, Drives Like a 26k-Mile Dream",
